.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Schrodinger&#x27;s first 1926 paper solved his wave equation for the hydrogen atom and recovered the Bohr energies E_n\propto-1/n^2 without any quantization postulate. HydrogenLikeAtom reproduces the textbook 13.6 eV hydrogen ground-state energy exactly, and its radial wavefunctions (built from the associated Laguerre polynomials of scipy.special) are independently checked here by numerically integrating them to a normalized probability of 1.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/hydrogenlike/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_hydrogen_orbitals_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hydrogenlike/plot_01_hydrogen_orbitals`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Schrodinger's hydrogen atom: radial wavefunctions and orbital energies</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/hydrogenlike/plot_01_hydrogen_orbitals`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Plots the first few 1D particle-in-a-box wavefunctions (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.particle_in_box.ParticleInBox1D), each offset by its own energy level, then checks the textbook 3D cubic-box degeneracy pattern (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.particle_in_box.ParticleInBox3D). These are the simplest exact solutions of Schrodinger&#x27;s 1926 time-independent equation -\frac{\hbar^2}{2m}\nabla^2\psi=E\psi inside hard walls: quantized energies E_n=n^2h^2/8mL^2 appear from the boundary conditions alone, with no quantization postulate.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_particle_in_box_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_01_particle_in_box`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Solving Schrodinger's equation for a particle in a box</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_01_particle_in_box`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Hans Kuhn (1949) treated the pi electrons of a linear conjugated dye as free electrons in a one-dimensional box as long as the conjugated chain. With N pi electrons filling the box levels two at a time, the lowest absorption is the HOMO to LUMO jump, \Delta E=\frac{h^2}{8mL^2}(N+1), so a longer chain (more electrons and a longer box) absorbs at longer wavelength. conjugated_dye_absorption_wavelength implements this on top of ParticleInBox1D.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Kuhn's free-electron model of conjugated dye color</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/particle_in_box/plot_02_kuhn_free_electron_dye`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Dennison (1926) solved Schrodinger&#x27;s equation for a rotating diatomic molecule, giving levels E_J=J(J+1)\hbar^2/2I. Builds an HCl-like RigidRotor from its atomic masses and bond length (~chemistrykit.quantum.systems.rigid_rotor.RigidRotor.from_diatomic), plots its energy-level diagram (each level&#x27;s height showing its (2J+1)-fold degeneracy), and shows that the J\to J+1 microwave absorption lines are evenly spaced by 2B.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/quantum/rigid_rotor/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_rigid_rotor_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/rigid_rotor/plot_01_rigid_rotor`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Dennison's rigid rotor: rotational levels and the microwave spectrum</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/quantum/rigid_rotor/plot_01_rigid_rotor`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Dennison (1926) gave the quantum-mechanical rotational energy levels E_J=\hbar^2J(J+1)/(2I). With the \Delta J=+1 selection rule, these levels produce absorption lines at 2B(J+1), which are evenly spaced by 2B. This example builds an HCl-like rigid rotor from chemistrykit.quantum, prints its level energies and line positions, checks the constant 2B spacing, and adds the Boltzmann-population intensity pattern seen in a real far-infrared or microwave spectrum.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/spectro/rotational/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_rotational_spectrum_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/spectro/rotational/plot_01_rotational_spectrum`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Dennison's quantized rigid rotor: HCl rotational lines spaced by 2B, with Boltzmann intensities</div>
